Optimal number N of descriptors were determined using MC: multicollinearity and RFECV: recursive feature extraction with tenfold cross-validation.

We detected novelties/outliers from our datasets by applying univariate or multivariate outlier detection methods. For univariate detection, we detected the outliers using Mahalanobis distance (MD)41. MD is an effective distance metric that measures the distance between a point and a distribution in multivariate space, i.e. 56 sequence-based physicochemical properties. The formula to compute Mahalanobis distance is as follows (Eq. (7)):7\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
				\usepackage{amsmath}
				\usepackage{wasysym} 
				\usepackage{amsfonts} 
				\usepackage{amssymb} 
				\usepackage{amsbsy}
				\usepackage{mathrsfs}
				\usepackage{upgreek}
				\setlength{\oddsidemargin}{-69pt}
				\begin{document}$$D^{2} = \left( {x - m} \right)^{T} \times C^{ - 1} \times \left( {x - m} \right)$$\end{document}D2=x-mT×C-1×x-mwhere D2 is the squared Mahalanobis distance, x is the input vector (row in a dataset), m is the vector of mean values of independent variables (mean of each column) and C−1 is the inverse covariance matrix of independent variables.

We measured the enrichment of 5000 generated inliers sequences in charged, small and bulky amino acids using differential cumulative frequencies of selected amino acids as follows in Fig. 7c (Eq. (8)) and Fig. 7d (Eq. (9)). Similar amino acid analysis was conducted for the 3081 peptides from APD dataset as shown in Figure S5.8\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
